[00:01] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Is this the future of farming? 170 cattle are looked after around the clock…by robots. The animals are fed four times a day. AI calculates exactly how much they need. Feeding systems like these cost around'

[00:14] [scene:demo]
VISUAL: An overhead WASSBAUER system distributing straw to cows resting in a pen.
AUDIO: '€100,000…but they pay off quickly. Farmhands are hard to find, and in the long run, robots are cheaper. Scattered straw helps the cows feel comfortable.'

[00:26] [scene:demo]
VISUAL: Close-up of a WASSBAUER machine processing and delivering feed.
AUDIO: 'Outside, feed is processed and delivered to the animals fully automatically. The farmer spends more time on his cell phone than in the barn. He controls and monitors his robots via an app.'

[00:41] [scene:interview]
VISUAL: Lukas Jettinger, a farmer, speaking about his experience.
AUDIO: '"I’ve got different rations for different animals: Cows, heifers, dry cows, bulls, young stock, transition feed. Seven feeding plans.'

[00:53] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'And the system handles all of it. If you did that with a manual mixer, you’d need someone working three hours a day." The milking robot, in particular,'

[01:05] [scene:demo]
VISUAL: Robotic milking equipment attaching to a cow's udder.
AUDIO: 'makes the work easier and reduces the number of employees needed. Sensors guide the milking equipment to the udder. The cows have quickly got used to it. At least four times a day, the cows go to the milking station'

[01:18] [scene:interview]
VISUAL: Lukas Jettinger, identified as a cattle rancher and dairy farmer, speaking.
AUDIO: 'on their own. “Without the tech, this farm wouldn’t survive. The workload would be too much: you’d need extra help every day, even on weekends.'

[01:31] [scene:scene]
VISUAL: Lukas Jettinger interacting with a cow in the barn.
AUDIO: 'At some point you start wondering: what’s the point? You want a life too. And as long as the tech works and it’s still fun, it’s great.”'

[01:44] [scene:scene]
VISUAL: Aerial view of a large farm complex.
AUDIO: 'There are no employees left in this cattle barn. The farmer runs it entirely on his own. But what if the equipment fails? He can\'t handle the work alone.'

[01:56] [scene:interview]
VISUAL: A woman smiling in an office setting, likely a farm manager or owner.
AUDIO: 'The farmer\'s technology comes, among other sources, from this company in Austria. It not only makes feeding robots but also offers round the clock service, just like other robot manufacturers who cater for farmers.'

[02:10] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: '“We can access any device anywhere in the world, if the internet connection is stable. We can see exactly what each circuit board is doing. Or not doing.” Modern farms are highly specialized.'

[02:23] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'This one in northern Germany houses 750 calves. While the farmer is out working the fields, he can still keep an eye on his calves via an online platform.'

[02:36] [scene:scene]
VISUAL: A farmer inside a tractor, looking at a screen.
AUDIO: 'His smartphone provides twentyfour seven updates: especially important, because calves are vulnerable to illness and need close monitoring! Right now, he’s getting an alert:'

[02:54] [scene:scene]
VISUAL: Calves moving around in a pen.
AUDIO: 'calf number 101 isn’t drinking enough milk. The data comes from a sensor on the calf’s ear. The animal doesn’t seem sick: it bolts at first sight. The farmer guides it to the feeding station:'

[03:08] [scene:interview]
VISUAL: A farmer speaking in the calf barn.
AUDIO: 'is it sick or just not hungry? “This calf just isn’t hungry right now, and doesn’t feel like drinking.'

[03:20] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Otherwise, it seems healthy. I’ll check back in two hours and keep an eye on it to see if it drinks the rest of its milk.” The calves are fed by an automated dispenser filled'

[03:34] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'with milk powder. If a calf doesn’t drink enough, the system flags it right away. The dispenser is equipped with sensors that measure how much…and how fast…a calf drinks. The data is then analyzed by AI, allowing the farmer to monitor'

[03:46] [scene:demo]
VISUAL: A hand interacting with controls on the automated calf feeder.
AUDIO: 'each calf’s health in detail. “This machine lets us improve animal welfare in the barn.'

[04:04] [scene:interview]
VISUAL: A farmer speaking in the calf barn.
AUDIO: 'It gives the calf twentyfour hour access to milk, so its routine mimics natural behavior. It can choose when and how often it feeds…many small meals, or a few bigger ones.”'

[04:19] [scene:demo]
VISUAL: A "FLYPIT" machine distributing feed from an overhead system.
AUDIO: 'More and more farms are handing over their heavy routine work to robots. The goal is to keep farming sustainable…and appealing for the next generation. And the animals?'

[04:33] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'They don’t seem to mind.'
